          "content": [
            "# Virtual Memory in 5 Bullet Points\n\n- **What it is:** Virtual memory gives each process its own large, private address space (e.g., \"your program can use addresses 0 to 2⁶⁴\"), decoupled from the physical RAM actually installed. The OS and hardware (MMU) translate virtual addresses to real physical addresses on the fly.\n\n- **Paging:** Virtual address space is split into fixed-size chunks called *pages* (typically 4 KB); physical RAM is divided into matching *frames*. A page table maps each virtual page to a frame (or marks it \"not in memory\"). This lets pages be scattered anywhere in RAM and enables memory protection between processes.\n\n- **Page faults:** When your program accesses a page not currently in RAM, the MMU raises a *page fault*. The OS pauses your thread, loads the page from disk (swap file or file-backed storage), updates the page table, and restarts the instruction. This is why memory can exceed physical RAM — but frequent faults to disk are slow, so the OS tries to keep hot pages resident.\n\n- **TLB (Translation Lookaside Buffer):** A small, ultra-fast cache in the CPU that stores recent virtual→physical translations. Without it, every memory access would need a page table lookup (possibly multiple, since page tables are themselves hierarchical). A TLB hit takes ~1 cycle; a miss can cost dozens to hundreds of cycles.\n\n- **Why you care as a programmer:** Locality matters — accessing memory sequentially or reusing recently-touched data keeps TLB/page caches happy. Big sparse jumps across huge arrays can trigger a storm of page faults and TLB misses. Also, allocation failures and swapping can make your program stall unpredictably, so watch memory usage in performance profiling."
          ],
